## Why This Is Asked

Assesses your ability to design an extensible data pipeline architecture that remains stable as new formats are added, using registries and factories to achieve loose coupling.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern for pluggable parser implementations
- Registry pattern to map file extensions to appropriate parsers
- Factory pattern to instantiate parsers at runtime
- Interface segregation with minimal, focused contracts

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Parser {
  parse(data: string): Record<string, any>[];
}

interface ParserFactory {
  create(): Parser;
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, factory) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, factory);
  }

  getParser(extension) {
    const factory = this.parsers.get(extension);
    return factory ? factory.create() : null;
  }
}

class Importer {
  constructor(registry) {
    this.registry = registry;
  }

  async import(filePath, data) {
    const extension = filePath.split('.').pop();
    const parser = this.registry.getParser(extension);

    if (!parser) {
      throw new Error(`No parser found for .${extension} files`);
    }

    return parser.parse(data);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('csv', () => new CsvParser());
registry.register('json', () => new JsonParser());
registry.register('tsv', () => new TsvParser());

const importer = new Importer(registry);
```

## Implementation Benefits

- **Extensibility**: New formats added without modifying core importer
- **Decoupling**: Registry isolates format-specific logic
- **Type Safety**: Interfaces ensure consistent parser contracts
- **Runtime Flexibility**: Parsers instantiated based on file extension

